   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{0}^{x^{2}} \\sqrt{t + 5} \\, dt \\). Find the exact value of \\( g'\\left(2\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 12
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, the derivative of the integral with respect to its upper limit is the integrand evaluated at that limit, \\( \\sqrt{x^{2} + 5} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Sqrt",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "x",
                  2
               ],
               5
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The upper limit is \\( x^{2} \\), so the chain rule multiplies by its derivative: \\( g'(x) = \\sqrt{x^{2} + 5} \\cdot 2 x \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            "x",
            [
               "Sqrt",
               [
                  "Add",
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "x",
                     2
                  ],
                  5
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = 2 \\) the limit is \\( 4 \\) and its derivative is \\( 4 \\).",
         "mathjson": 4
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( g'\\left(2\\right) = 3 \\cdot 4 = 12 \\).",
         "mathjson": 12
      }
   ],
